  "slug": "gene-rna-d705",
  "summary": "Single-cell, spatial and genetic screens now link germline, meiotic and ovarian regulators to transposon control and gamete quality.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how specific regulators sustain gene expression programs and genome integrity during gametogenesis. In mouse germ cells, COPS5 loss downregulates piRNA pathway genes, reduces pachytene piRNAs, activates LINE1 and IAP transposons, and arrests spermatogenesis. In budding yeast gametogenesis, Hsf1, Skn7, Msn2 and Msn4 are active without external stress, and the antioxidant genes TSA1 and AHP1 protect gametes. In Drosophila, Marf1 is newly expressed in the male accessory gland and integrates into its gene regulatory network, while GAR1 regulates lipid storage, development, sgg splicing and PI3K/AKT signaling. Single-cell and spatial multi-omics of human ovaries across menopause implicate HEYL in ovarian aging and senescence, and a yeast synthetic genetic array screen links nuclear genes to mitochondrial COX2 expression via Rim101 signaling. These last two extend the scope beyond germline silencing.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
